### Description

Hello!

I've noticed that the new ResourceSnapshot union type declares that whenever there is an error, there can never be a value also.

This is incorrect from a logical perspective. A resource can successfully load a value, then encounter an error on a reload, and thus have both a (preserved) value and an error - similar to how a resource can be loading data but already have a value as demonstrated by the `withPreviousValue` example in the docs.

It is a common UI pattern to show the latest succesfully loaded data AND show an error message that the reload was unsuccessful.
```
@if (myResource.error()) {
    <p>There was an error</p>
}
@if (myResource.hasValue()) {
    <p>Here's the latest known data</p>
    ...
}
```

The ResourceSnapshot type prevents this UI pattern from being conveniently implemented it seems.

### Proposed solution

Make the ResourceSnapshot this type instead, to allow for a previous value to be exposed even when there is an error:
```
type ResourceSnapshot<T> = {
    readonly status: "idle";
    readonly value: T;
} | {
    readonly status: "loading" | "reloading";
    readonly value: T;
} | {
    readonly status: "resolved" | "local";
    readonly value: T;
} | {
    readonly status: "error";
    readonly error: Error;
} | {
    readonly status: "error";
    readonly error: Error;
    readonly value: T;
}
```

### Alternatives considered

We're currently using a (very) custom implementation of withPreviousValue using Proxy and Reflect, due to the limitations of ResourceSnapshot. I'd rather use the ResourceSnapshot pattern. Our migration is also hindered by the fact that `reload()` disappears from the WriteableResource when you use resourceFromSnapshots, but that's beside the main point of this issue.

